{"id": "62842a30-bf88-4a16-a831-3605857739d2", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "There's one associated account, 123456789012. I'll have it scanned for Bedrock keys now. That means checking every IAM user's service-specific credentials, which pulls in a lot of data.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_nmcGigUlTIeb6ZcGfgMNry", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-key-audit", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "[DEEP RESEARCH MODE]
{
  "task": "Produce a complete inventory and risk assessment of every long-term Amazon Bedrock API key in the account. READ-ONLY: do not modify or delete anything.",
  "context": "Long-term Bedrock API keys are IAM service-specific credentials with ServiceName bedrock.amazonaws.com, attached to IAM users (often auto-created users named like BedrockAPIKey-xxxx). The user wants a full report and a recommendation on which keys should be removed. Only AWS account 123456789012 is associated (us-east-1 agent space); IAM is global.",
  "resources": ["AWS_ACCOUNT:123456789012", "IAM users", "iam:ListServiceSpecificCredentials"],
  "timeframe": "Current state as of 2026-11-20T09:24:00Z",
  "criteria": "1) Enumerate ALL IAM users (paginate) and call list_service_specific_credentials with ServiceName=bedrock.amazonaws.com for each (also try without ServiceName filter and report only bedrock ones). For each key capture: user name, ServiceSpecificCredentialId, ServiceUserName, Status (Active/Inactive/Expired), CreateDate, ExpirationDate (if any; note keys with no expiry / 'never expires'). 2) For each owning user collect: user creation date, attached managed policies and inline policies (flag AmazonBedrockLimitedAccess vs broad such as AdministratorAccess, bedrock:*, iam:* , * on *), groups, permissions boundary, console login profile, regular IAM access keys, MFA, tags. 3) Usage: PasswordLastUsed, and for each key any last-used evidence available (iam get_user / get_access_key_last_used where applicable; service last accessed via generate_service_last_accessed_details / get_service_last_accessed_details for the user for Bedrock services; CloudTrail via lookup_cloudtrail_events for CreateServiceSpecificCredential / ResetServiceSpecificCredential / UpdateServiceSpecificCredential / DeleteServiceSpecificCredential and for bedrock InvokeModel/Converse calls by that user over the last 90 days, if available). Note clearly where last-used data is unavailable. 4) Classify each key with a recommendation of REMOVE / REVIEW / KEEP with reasons, using: inactive or expired status, no expiration set, age >90 days, unused for 30+ days or never used, overly broad permissions on the owning user, owner user with no other purpose (dedicated BedrockAPIKey-* user), human user with console access, creator unknown. 5) Also check whether any SCP/IAM policy denies iam:CreateServiceSpecificCredential if easily visible. Return a structured report: summary counts, per-key table with all fields above, per-key recommendation and reasoning, and any data gaps/permission errors encountered."
}", "preloaded_skills": ["investigating-infrastructure-changes"]}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit long-term Bedrock API keys across IAM users"}], "created_at": null}

